<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#FBF8F1">

  <title>
    {%- if template.name == 'index' -%}
      {{ shop.name }} - Italian Artisan Coffee, Imported Without Compromise
    {%- elsif page_title == blank -%}
      {{ shop.name }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ page_title }} - {{ shop.name }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </title>

  {%- comment -%}
    META DESCRIPTION — authored HERE, not in the Shopify admin (Steve, 2026-08-19).

    This is the grey sentence under a Google result: the first thing a stranger reads about
    the company, before they have seen anything we made. It was coming from the admin SEO
    field and read "Crema Italia - curated italian roasted coffee only found here." — lowercase
    on the one word the whole proposition rests on, 62 characters of an available ~155, and
    "only found here" claiming exclusivity when the differentiator is that the coffee is
    UNCHANGED.

    It drifted for months without anyone noticing, and the reason is worth keeping: the
    coming-soon theme HARDCODES its own description (live-theme/layout/theme.liquid:36), so
    the live site read correctly while the admin value sat wrong behind it. Nothing reviews an
    admin field. Code gets diffed.

    The fallback chain below is the one live-theme already uses, ported:
      index          -> this theme-authored line (brand copy we own)
      page_description -> the record's own SEO field (products, pages, articles own theirs)
      neither        -> back to this line rather than emitting nothing
    Note the POC is a single-document SPA, so template.name is always 'index' and the first
    branch always wins here. The other two are for production, where real templates exist.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ci_meta_desc = 'Freshly roasted in Italy, air-freighted whole bean, and sold exactly as the roaster sealed it. Coffee from a small, named group of Italian artisan roasters.' -%}
  {%- if template.name == 'index' -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ ci_meta_desc | escape }}">
  {%- elsif page_description -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
  {%- else -%}
    <meta name="description" content="{{ ci_meta_desc | escape }}">
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Open Graph / Twitter card, added 2026-08-18. This theme had NONE, while the
    coming-soon page it replaces (live-theme/layout/theme.liquid) has nine - so every
    share of the real storefront rendered as a bare grey link while the placeholder
    rendered a proper card. For a brand that grows by someone texting a friend a link
    to a roaster, that is the cheapest conversion asset on the list, and the image was
    already sitting unreferenced in assets/ at a textbook 1200x630.

    Copy is aligned to the current hero (freshness first). Absolute https URL is forced
    on the image: asset_url returns a protocol-relative //cdn.shopify.com path, and some
    scrapers will not follow that.

    PROD: this is a single-document SPA, so one static card covers every route. On the
    real storefront these become per-template - product pages want the product title,
    its own photograph, og:type=product, and price/availability - plus Product and
    Organization JSON-LD, which this theme still has none of.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ci_og_title = 'Crema Italia - Freshly roasted in Italy' -%}
  {%- assign ci_og_desc = 'Whole-bean coffee from a small, named group of artisan Italian roasters. Air-freighted so it reaches you weeks from the roast date, not months.' -%}
  {%- comment -%} asset_url returns a protocol-relative //host/... path; OG scrapers need an
      absolute one. The conditional keeps this correct if Shopify ever returns absolute. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ci_asset = 'ci-og-image.png' | asset_url -%}
  {%- if ci_asset contains 'http' -%}{%- assign ci_og_image = ci_asset -%}
  {%- else -%}{%- assign ci_og_image = ci_asset | prepend: 'https:' -%}{%- endif -%}
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ shop.name }}">
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{ ci_og_title }}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ ci_og_desc }}">
  <meta property="og:image" content="{{ ci_og_image }}">
  <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
  <meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="Crema Italia - Italian artisan coffee">
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{ shop.url }}">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ ci_og_title }}">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ ci_og_desc }}">
  <meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ ci_og_image }}">

  {%- comment -%}
    STRUCTURED DATA (JSON-LD), added 2026-08-18 (POC15). The theme had NONE, which is why
    the site cannot appear as anything but a plain blue link: no knowledge panel, no logo
    beside the result, and no route to star ratings later.

    WHAT IS DEMONSTRABLY TRUE TODAY is emitted. Everything else is deliberately absent,
    and the absences matter as much as the presences:

      - NO potentialAction / SearchAction. This site has no search - the header search
        icon was removed in POC9 because it promised a field and only routed to Shop.
        Declaring a search endpoint we do not have is a false claim to a crawler.
      - NO sameAs. No social profiles exist yet. An invented sameAs is worse than none.
      - NO contactPoint. info@ and support@ are not created yet (CLAUDE.md §10 open
        items). We do not publish a channel nobody is reading.
      - NO Product or AggregateRating. This is a single-document SPA: every "page" is a
        div toggled by showPage(), so there is exactly one URL and no per-product address
        for a Product node to point at. Emitting Product markup here would either name
        URLs that 404 or describe a product a crawler never sees, and there are no reviews
        to aggregate in any case. Product + AggregateRating belong on real per-product
        templates in production - specified in docs/production_build_spec.md §9, which is
        also the route to stars in search results.

    Address is town/state/country only. We have not published a street address and do not
    invent one here.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign ci_logo_asset = 'ci-logo.png' | asset_url -%}
  {%- if ci_logo_asset contains 'http' -%}{%- assign ci_logo_url = ci_logo_asset -%}
  {%- else -%}{%- assign ci_logo_url = ci_logo_asset | prepend: 'https:' -%}{%- endif -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#organization' | json }},
        "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
        "legalName": "Crema Italia, LLC",
        "url": {{ shop.url | json }},
        "description": {{ ci_og_desc | json }},
        "logo": {
          "@type": "ImageObject",
          "url": {{ ci_logo_url | json }},
          "width": 600,
          "height": 181
        },
        "image": {{ ci_og_image | json }},
        "address": {
          "@type": "PostalAddress",
          "addressLocality": "Lutz",
          "addressRegion": "FL",
          "addressCountry": "US"
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "WebSite",
        "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#website' | json }},
        "url": {{ shop.url | json }},
        "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
        "inLanguage": "en-US",
        "publisher": { "@id": {{ shop.url | append: '/#organization' | json }} }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="{{ 'ci-favicon-32.png' | asset_url }}">
  <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="16x16" href="{{ 'ci-favicon-16.png' | asset_url }}">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" sizes="180x180" href="{{ 'ci-apple-touch-icon.png' | asset_url }}">

  <!-- Google Fonts: Marcellus (display) + Inter (body). Rebrand 2026-07-01: display = Marcellus (web stand-in for logo font Montecatini Pro).
       Inter now loads its REAL italic (ital axis). Before 2026-08-18 only roman was loaded, so every
       italic on the site was a browser-synthesised oblique - a mechanically slanted roman. Marcellus
       has NO italic and only weight 400: never set font-style:italic or a weight >400 on the display
       face, or the browser fakes it. See the corrective block at the foot of ci-storefront.css. -->
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
  <link
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Marcellus&family=Inter:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;1,400;1,500;1,600&display=swap"
    rel="stylesheet"
  >

  {{ content_for_header }}

  {{ 'ci-storefront.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

  <!-- POC3: baked-in test dataset location for the storefront JS. -->
  <script>window.CI_CATALOG_URL = {{ 'ci-catalog.json' | asset_url | json }};</script>
  <!-- Resolves catalog `people[].photo` filenames to real asset URLs for the JS-rendered
       person page. PROD: real team/partner records carry their own image URLs. -->
  <script>window.CI_ASSETS = {
    "ci-lucia.jpg": {{ 'ci-lucia.jpg' | asset_url | json }},
    "ci-asia.jpg": {{ 'ci-asia.jpg' | asset_url | json }}
  };</script>
</head>

<body>
  {% render 'ci-header' %}
  {% render 'ci-profile-banner' %}

  {{ content_for_layout }}

  {% render 'ci-store-footer' %}
  <div class="flag-strip flag-bottom" aria-hidden="true"><div></div><div></div><div></div></div>

  {% render 'ci-quiz-modal' %}
  {% render 'ci-signin-modal' %}
  {% render 'ci-taste-console' %}
  <div class="toast" id="ci-toast" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>

  <script src="{{ 'ci-storefront.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
